# Rufus Linux preview @RUFUS_PREVIEW_REVISION@

This is Linux preview @RUFUS_PREVIEW_REVISION@, based on Rufus @RUFUS_UPSTREAM_VERSION@. It is a
development port with a working Qt6 desktop interface and native Linux media
operations. Complete Windows Rufus feature parity remains in development.

CP78 / Preview47 prepares the useful x86-64 release: original Rufus translations
are reused across 38 languages; Linux-specific text may remain English. Native
Debian/Fedora packages and an Arch AUR source recipe join Arch/CachyOS and generic
install trees. See the [release evidence](docs/linux-release-evidence.md) and
[package instructions](linux/packaging/README.md).

Existing UDF1.02–2.01 remains supported. UDF2.50/2.60, ReFS formatting and Dev
Drive detection are user-deferred. The actual Windows UDF indexing attribute is
unavailable. Full native translations, additional application host architectures,
non-x64 To Go and unavailable physical USB/4Kn checks remain follow-ups; complete
Windows parity is not claimed.

CP76 / Preview45 delivered the [sector and filesystem geometry batch](docs/linux-sector-geometry.md):
512/512e/4Kn discovery/request/layout/formatter geometry, FAT32 64 KiB clusters,
UDF logical blocks and exact provider limits.

CP75 / Preview44 delivered the [legacy ISO routes batch](docs/linux-legacy-iso-routes.md).

CP74 / Preview43 delivered the [ISO/EFI resource batch](docs/linux-iso-efi-resources.md):
all eight source fallback architectures, embedded EFI/repair, Windows NTFS and FAT/UDF
link rules, image-matched Syslinux resources/old modules, Slax/mastering recovery,
portable native accessors and local cached issuer metadata. The current completion
register records the remaining work.

CP73 / Preview42 delivered the [device/source operations batch](docs/linux-device-source-operations.md):
raw optical ISO capture, USB reset/ESP toggling, exclusion settings, file-backed
virtual devices, source/home backing resolution, session locking override and
explicit parent-image relocation. The completion register records current progress. See the [completion plan](docs/linux-release-plan.md).

Earlier previews added running-application disk exclusion and mounted extended USB
labels, including Multiple Volumes captions. Discovery stays unprivileged and
uses existing mounts; see [native device discovery](docs/linux-device-discovery.md).

SELECT/DOWNLOAD now includes the native Windows/UEFI Shell chooser, browser
handoff or streamed image saving and automatic selection after cleanup. See
[native image downloads](docs/linux-image-downloads.md) for the signed provider,
cancellation and native filesystem publication/recovery behavior.

The language button now offers all 38 original catalog languages with saved
explicit selection, `-l` / `--locale`, live relabeling and right-to-left layout.
The initial choice is English; native-only text without a matching original
translation still appears in English. See [application languages](docs/linux-localization.md).

Settings now checks native application releases alongside Secure Boot policies.
Existing Secure Boot consent stays narrow; automatic application checks require
an explicit choice. The beta preference and Alt+Y forced check are available.
Updates open the publisher's release page for package-manager installation. See
[native application updates](docs/linux-application-updates.md), including the
preview channel and package-manager installation. Enable beta releases to see this preview.



## CP70 — NTFS compression and indexing (Preview 39)

The [NTFS options batch](docs/linux-ntfs-options.md) groups session Alt+N compression,
saved Alt+Q indexing, frozen NTFS-only options, native formatter defaults and
boot-file compression exceptions. Larger clusters continue uncompressed with
a diagnostic. Compressed file creation preselects the qualified NTFS-3G provider
before writes; ordinary NTFS creation keeps the kernel provider. No write or
corruption error switches drivers. The guide records native directory-default
restoration, provider evidence and remaining UDF/ReFS indexing-hint work.

## Install and start

On Arch or CachyOS, install the generated native package with the package manager:

```sh
sudo pacman -U rufus-linux-preview-@RUFUS_UPSTREAM_VERSION@.preview@RUFUS_PREVIEW_REVISION@-1-@CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR@.pkg.tar.zst
rufus-linux
```

The native package is built against the Arch/CachyOS runtime on the build host.
The separate `.tar.gz` contains an install tree for packagers and uses system
libraries; it is not a self-contained portable application.

Run the GUI as your desktop user. Select an image and device, review the target
confirmation, then approve the normal desktop authorization prompt. The same
helper completes the job and cleanup. Successful READY means writes,
verification, sync, cleanup and helper exit have finished.

Raw DD needs the application, UDisks2 and polkit. Other modes use optional
filesystem tools listed by the native package. A working desktop polkit agent
is required. The package includes matching native Syslinux 4.07 and 6.04-pre1
installers when those sources were selected at build time.

Parent images use caller-owned, owner-readable files and portable relative
locators. VHDX log replay uses an immutable in-memory overlay. See the support
guide for parent relocation and replay limits.

Full single-store FFU reading includes LZNT1 and Huffman-XPRESS, grouped
compressed buffers and multi-block end-relative runs. Full FFU captures retain
all GPT names in the original GPT payload. FFU saving uses desktop-compatible plain XPRESS
compression with bounded literal expansion and explicit zero-region restoration. Optimized
FFUs fit smaller/larger targets with NTFS growth and recovery-location updates.
V2 store selection and desktop catalog signature policy are supported. CP56
closes the [FFU feature audit](docs/linux-ffu-parity.md).
Partial single-store updates compare every declared base range before any
writes, preserve omitted bytes and apply their metadata as declared.

Hold **Ctrl** while pressing **SELECT** to attach a supplementary ZIP after
selecting the image. It is extracted after file-based creation/formatting in the
same authorized job, with source protection, CRC checks and sync before READY.
The confirmation shows the archive. DD and ext modes omit it. See the
[supplementary ZIP guide](docs/linux-supplementary-zip.md).

Use SELECT, drop a local image onto the window, or start with
`rufus-linux -i /path/to/image` (`--iso` or one positional path also works).
Alt+M saves an explicit boot-marker override for the next image selection.
It retains all source identity, geometry, target and readback checks; a marker
or an override does not establish that an image will boot.

Full/quick formatting is now selectable for eligible native filesystems.
Full FAT clears and verifies the volume; NTFS/exFAT use native full modes;
ext3/ext4 fully initialize journals, including persistence. Large FAT32 stays
quick-only. Long full jobs remain cancelable and wait for cleanup/helper exit.
See the support guide for filesystem differences and qualification limits.

Enable **Runtime UEFI media validation** for eligible ISO copies to validate
checksums at boot before starting the original EFI loader. Existing lists retain
unchanged source digests; Rufus repairs entries it modifies and includes split
WIM parts. The bundled validators are installed in the same authorized job.
See the [runtime validation guide](docs/linux-runtime-validation.md). This does
not authenticate the image publisher or replace Secure Boot trust checks.

The image reader identifies Secure Boot issuers and checks reported EFI
loaders against Rufus's bundled DBX/SBAT/SVN and certificate policies. A revoked
loader produces the original START-time warning before authorization, including
for hybrid DD creation. See the [Secure Boot guide](docs/linux-secure-boot.md)
for the distinction between metadata classification and firmware trust. Settings now supplies opt-in
policy checks, session list refresh, scheduled DBX checks and confirmed atomic
DBX caching. Select the image again to use updated policy; see the
[policy update guide](docs/linux-policy-updates.md).

MS-DOS is available on MBR/FAT16/FAT32 with explicit keyboard/code-page choices
and a consented, hash-checked Microsoft resource download/cache. The resource
is read as data; Microsoft files are not bundled in these packages. Advanced
ReactOS prepares its original boot records for separately supplied system files.
See the [legacy boot guide](docs/linux-legacy-boot.md).

## Available workflows

- Raw disk images and gzip, XZ, BZIP2, LZMA, LZW, ZIP, Zstd, VTSI and fixed or
  dynamic VHD/VHDX disk images, with readback before successful completion.
- Full FFU images, including explicit V2 store selection, with uncompressed, LZNT1, plain XPRESS or
  Huffman-XPRESS payloads, deployed to an equal or larger disk with GPT relocation.
  Partial FFUs update a matching existing disk after all selected-store base comparisons pass.
  V2 containers authenticate every store and deploy only the explicitly selected store.
  Optimized full FFUs resize the designated NTFS partition and repair recovery locations
  before successful READY; ntfsresize is required and dirty/corrupt captures are refused.
- Live USB persistence for compatible Syslinux/GRUB images, with ext2/ext3/ext4,
  size controls and Debian/Casper boot configuration. See the support guide for
  tested examples and distribution limits.
- Save a device to fixed VHD, dynamic VHDX or full XPRESS-compressed FFU,
  with automatic source unmount, verified readback, source consistency checks
  and atomic output publication. FFU capture supports empty GPT disks,
  multi-chunk manifests and GPT entry tables up to 4 MiB.
- Export filesystem files as UDF-only ISO images with optional cdrtools, preserving
  hidden/Unicode files and verifying the complete tree before publication.
- Windows To Go from Windows ISO or standalone WIM/ESD, with native NTFS metadata,
  archive/edition selection, GPT or MBR boot setup and installed policy. See the
  [To Go guide](docs/linux-windows-to-go.md) for qualification limits.
- Supported Windows installation media, WIM splitting and implemented Windows
  customization options.
- Supported Linux ISO creation with EFI, Syslinux, GRUB2 and GRUB4DOS routes.
- ISO → ESP for eligible small regular-EFI images on GPT/FAT16 or GPT/FAT32,
  including compatible persistence and the original formatter-default rules.
- FAT16/FAT32, NTFS, exFAT, UDF and advanced ext2/ext3 data formatting on MBR,
  GPT and eligible SFD layouts. UDF requires udftools and supports revisions
  1.02/1.50/2.00/2.01. See the [formatting guide](docs/linux-filesystem-formatting.md)
  for provider and geometry limits; later UDF revisions and ReFS remain open.
- The original old-BIOS alignment and compatibility-partition option for
  eligible MBR/BIOS media, with UEFI:NTFS and persistence priority preserved.
- Full device zeroing with Alt+Z and Windows-compatible fast clearing with
  Ctrl+Alt+Z, one helper and readback before completion. No filesystem is created.
- Implemented standalone boot loaders, checksums, logs and device filtering.

Read the [supported media guide](docs/linux-support.md) in the installed
documentation for exact image,
filesystem, geometry and provider boundaries. The
[parity backlog](docs/linux-feature-parity.md) lists unfinished features,
including localization, native updates/download selection,
Windows-specific parent relocation and other
advanced and legacy functions. Representative guest tests do not establish
compatibility with every physical USB controller, firmware or operating system.

`rufus-linux --version` and `rufus-linux --help` work without a display. The
About button includes the original GPL license and upstream credits. The log
can be saved from its dialog for troubleshooting. CP59 adds adjacent portable
INI storage, startup preferences, remembered advanced panels, automatic/persistent
logs and Ctrl+L/P, Alt+R/U. See the [desktop guide](docs/linux-desktop-settings.md).

Read-only USB devices are listed for image saving with write/format actions
disabled. Saving preserves kernel write protection and the existing source,
verification, transactional publication and cleanup checks.


## CP69 — ISO read options and timestamps (Preview 38)

The [ISO controls batch](docs/linux-iso-options.md) groups Alt+I/J/K/S/T: session
ISO/Joliet/Rock Ridge and size checks, saved timestamp preservation, coherent
selected-source analysis/preflight/extraction and the explicit MS-DOS 64 KiB
cluster override. Physical capacity and actual filesystem bounds remain enforced.
Ordinary ISO9660/UDF copies preserve access/modification times when enabled;
creation time uses native filesystem behavior. Directory times are applied after
children so copying cannot immediately overwrite them. See the guide for the
Windows behavior, Linux differences and qualification limits.


## CP71 — advanced device selectors (Preview 40)

Alt+G/Alt+W and Ctrl+Alt+F now control recognized native virtual, VMware and
non-USB removable disks; native MMC cards share the existing card filter.
Selected classes are frozen and independently revalidated by the helper.
See [native discovery](docs/linux-device-discovery.md) for exact provider scope,
protection and grouped qualification. The complete parity goal remains active.
